From: YSPEED firmware maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] gpio: start dynamic allocation at 480 to match vendor numbering

The YS-GPS-12832 stock firmware (MT7628) enumerates its three 32-line GPIO
banks from 480 downwards (ARCH_NR_GPIOS 512, allocated downwards) and all of
its userspace, including the display binary, assumes those numbers: 486 for
SPI CS, 494 reset, 496 D/C, 499 backlight. Mainline's dynamic allocator starts
at 512 instead, so the same pads appear as 518/526/528/531 and the vendor
binary cannot export its pins. Start dynamic allocation at 480.

--- a/include/linux/gpio.h
+++ b/include/linux/gpio.h
@@ -63,12 +63,16 @@
 /*
  * At the end we want all GPIOs to be dynamically allocated from 0.
  * However, some legacy drivers still perform fixed allocation.
- * Until they are all fixed, leave 0-512 space for them.
+ * The YS-GPS-12832 stock firmware (MT7628) numbers its three 32-line banks
+ * from 480 downwards, and the vendor userspace - including its display
+ * binary - assumes that numbering. Start dynamic allocation at 480 so the
+ * mainline kernel reproduces it; 0-480 stays reserved for legacy fixed
+ * allocation.
  */
-#define GPIO_DYNAMIC_BASE	512
+#define GPIO_DYNAMIC_BASE	480
 /*
  * Define the maximum of the possible GPIO in the global numberspace.
  * While the GPIO base and numbers are positive, we limit it with signed
  * maximum as a lot of code is using negative values for special cases.
  */
 #define GPIO_DYNAMIC_MAX	INT_MAX
